module

public import LeanPlayground.PerfectCuboid.ThirdConjectureModP

/-!
# The third cuboid polynomial is irreducible on explicit classes modulo `13 · 17`

An application of the two-prime criterion `irreducible_of_tables` of
`LeanPlayground.PerfectCuboid.ThirdConjectureModP` to the primes `13` and `17`.

The reduction of `cuboidThreePoly a b c` modulo `p` depends only on `(a², b², c²) mod p`. The tables
below list, for each residue class of `(a², b², c²)` with a useful factorization pattern, the
factorization of the reduction into monic irreducible factors; every factor is certified
irreducible by the kernel (`classOK`). The patterns used are

* modulo `13`: `{6, 6}` (`table13A`, 24 classes) and `{4, 8}` (`table13B`, 24 classes);
* modulo `17`: `{2, 10}` (`table17A`, 32 classes), `{1, 1, 2, 8}` (`table17B`, 32 classes) and
  `{6, 6}` (`table17C`, 96 classes).

The degrees of the divisors are the subset sums `{0, 6, 12}` and `{0, 4, 8, 12}` modulo `13`, and
`{0, 2, 10, 12}`, `{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12}` and `{0, 6, 12}` modulo `17`. The pairs
`13A × 17A`, `13A × 17B`, `13B × 17A`, `13B × 17C` only share `0` and `12`, so a factor over `ℤ`
is trivial.

**Main theorem** (`irreducible_cuboidThreePoly_of_mod_13_17`). If
`(a² mod 13, b² mod 13, c² mod 13)` is in `table13A` and `(a² mod 17, b² mod 17, c² mod 17)` is in
`table17A` or `table17B`, or the first is in `table13B` and the second in `table17A` or
`table17C`, then `cuboidThreePoly a b c` is irreducible in `ℤ[X]`. No positivity, coprimality or
distinctness of `a, b, c` is needed.

These classes contain `294912 / 10793861 ≈ 2.73%` of all triples `(a, b, c) mod 221`. One prime is
never enough (the constant term `(abc)⁴` is a square and the polynomial is even), and `2.96%` is the
most that the pair `(13, 17)` can give; other pairs give more (e.g. about `8.5%` for
`(19, 29)`; pairs with the smaller prime at most `23` were counted). Example: `(a, b, c) = (1, 2, 3)`
(`irreducible_cuboidThreePoly_one_two_three`).
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace PerfectCuboid.ThirdConjecture.ModP

open Polynomial

